WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 321: James II & VII: The Glorious Revolution of 1688 and the Battle of the Boyne

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:16.800
[Rebecca] Today, our Kings and Queens of England series examines the sovereign whose unbending religious zeal sparked a revolution that birthed modern constitutional democracy.

4
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.160
[Rebecca] The last Catholic monarch of Britain, King James I.

5
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:28.480
[Rebecca] Here is George with the dramatic story of 1688 and the Battle of the Boyne.

6
00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.280
[George] Born in October 1633 at St.

7
00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:44.800
[George] James's Palace, James Stewart, Duke of York, was endowed with personal bravery, rigorous administrative diligence, and a stiff, humorless obstinacy that stood in stark contrast to his charming brother Charles I.

8
00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:55.760
[George] Escaping parliamentary captivity during the Civil War disguised as a girl, James served with distinction as a professional cavalry officer under the French Marshal Tiren.

9
00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:15.040
[George] Following the Restoration in 1660, James served for 13 years as Lord High Admiral of the Royal Navy, demonstrating personal courage at sea during the Second and Third Anglo-Dutch Wars, commanding the fleet in the thick of battle at Loistoft in 1665 and Solbay in 1672.

10
00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:20.320
[George] But in the late 1660s, James secretly converted to Roman Catholicism.

11
00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:33.200
[George] When Parliament passed the Test Act in 1673, James refused to take the Anglican sacrament, resigning his naval command and marrying the 15 year old Italian Catholic Princess Mary of Modena.

12
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:46.880
[George] Surviving the virulent hysteria of the exclusion crisis under his brother's protection, James ascended the throne peacefully in February 1685, with the realm initially trusting his promise to defend the Church of England.

13
00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:54.240
[George] James's naval career was characterized by conspicuous personal courage and meticulous organizational diligence.

14
00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:07.080
[George] As Lord High Admiral, he modernized the naval administration, codified fighting instructions for fleet line of battle tactics, and championed the development of naval dockyards at Chatham and Portsmouth.

15
00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:16.840
[George] At the Battle of Sol Bay in 1672, when his flagship the Royal Prince was disabled by Dutch cannon fire, James transferred his flag to the St.

16
00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:28.760
[George] Michael under heavy fire, and when that vessel was battered, transferred again to the London, earning the fierce devotion of ordinary sailors who affectionately nicknamed him, Old Rowley's brother.

17
00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:34.440
[George] James's reign was immediately challenged in June 1685 by the Monmouth Rebellion.

18
00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:35.240
[George] Charles I.

19
00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:52.920
[George] Thousands of West Country clothworkers, miners and yeomen flocked to his standard, armed with scythes and pitchforks.

20
00:02:52.920 --> 00:03:07.800
[George] On July 6, 1685, at the Battle of Sedgemore in Somerset, the last pitched battle fought on English soil, James's regular army under the Earl of Feversham and John Churchill rooted the rebel pikemen in a nocturnal fog.

21
00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:15.240
[George] Monmouth was captured hiding in a ditch and beheaded on Tower Hill in a gruesome execution that took five blows of the axe.

22
00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:18.280
[George] James unleashed terrifying judicial vengeance.

23
00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:39.000
[George] Under the ruthless Lord Chief Justice George Jeffreys, the bloody assizes toured the West Country, sentencing over three hundred rebels to be hanged, drawn and quartered, their boiled heads and limbs impaled on crossroads across Somerset, while eight hundred more were transported into slavery in Barbados, horrifying the English nation.

24
00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:42.440
[George] Monmouth's rebellion was a tragic tragedy of errors.

25
00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:46.480
[George] Monmouth was a charismatic, handsome figure who claimed that Charles I.

26
00:03:52.080 --> 00:04:00.000
[George] But his poorly armed rebel army of West Country rustics was easily outmatched by the regular soldiers of the Royal Army at Sedgemore.

27
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.800
[George] The subsequent bloody ascises under Judge Jeffrey's horrified even royalists.

28
00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:19.280
[George] Jeffries roared at trembling prisoners from the bench, sentencing men to be hanged in batches and displaying their tarred corpses across western villages, fostering a deep, bitter hatred for James's regime that exploded three years later.

29
00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:27.200
[George] Emboldened by his victory over Monmouth, James made the fatal assumption that his subjects would meekly submit to his royal will.

30
00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:38.480
[George] He expanded his standing army to 30,000 men, quartering troops on Hounslow Heath to intimidate London, and placed Catholic officers in command in defiance of the Test Act.

31
00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:49.840
[George] In 1687, James issued the Declaration of Indulgence, using his royal dispensing power to unilaterally suspend all penal laws against Catholics and dissenters.

32
00:04:49.840 --> 00:05:01.680
[George] When the fellows of Magdalen College, Oxford, refused to elect a royal Catholic nominee as their president, James expelled them by force, converting the college into a Catholic seminary.

33
00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:08.160
[George] He appointed Jesuit Father Edward Petter to the Privy Council and welcomed a papal nuncio to Windsor.

34
00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:12.080
[George] James believed he was championing enlightened religious tolerance.

35
00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:27.680
[George] His subjects, however, watching Louis XIV's brutal dragonades persecuting Huguenots across the Channel following the revocation of the Edict of Nantes, viewed James's actions as a tyrannical conspiracy to impose French-style Catholic absolutism.

36
00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:33.400
[George] James's assault on English universities and corporations was a catastrophic political blunder.

37
00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:43.480
[George] By forcing Catholic fellows into Oxford colleges and dismissing Anglican justices of the peace and deputy lieutenants who refused to support the repeal of the Test Acts.

38
00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:55.560
[George] James alienated the natural bedrock of Stuart monarchy, the Tory country gentry and the Church of England clergy, when he appointed the Jesuit priest Father Edward Petter to his privy council.

39
00:05:55.560 --> 00:06:08.040
[George] Moderate English Catholics like Lord Balassi's warned the king that his reckless speed was driving his Protestant subjects into armed rebellion, but James stubbornly insisted that concession was the path to abdication.

40
00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:12.760
[George] In May 1688, James made a catastrophic miscalculation.

41
00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:19.560
[George] He ordered all Anglican clergy to read his declaration of indulgence aloud from their pulpits during Sunday service.

42
00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:30.040
[George] Seven prominent bishops, led by Archbishop of Canterbury William Sancroft, submitted a polite, respectful petition questioning the king's legal dispensing power.

43
00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:36.440
[George] James flew into a rage, accusing the bishops of seditious rebellion and locking them in the Tower of London.

44
00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:49.160
[George] When the seven bishops were tried in Westminster Hall in June, the jury delivered a verdict of not guilty, prompting wild, thunderous cheering across London and celebrations among the soldiers at Hounslow Heath.

45
00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:53.560
[George] Two days later, an explosive event altered the dynastic landscape.

46
00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:59.640
[George] Queen Mary of Modena gave birth to a healthy son, Prince James Francis Edward Stuart.

47
00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:06.920
[George] Until that moment, English Protestants had tolerated James, knowing his heirs were his Protestant daughters Mary and Anne.

48
00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:10.360
[George] Now, a permanent Catholic dynasty was born.

49
00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:20.400
[George] Panicked Protestants circulated a sensational conspiracy theory that the Queen had suffered a miscarriage, and that a strange baby had been smuggled into the bed in a brass warming pan.

50
00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:25.760
[George] The trial of the seven bishops in Westminster Hall was the moral climax of the reign.

51
00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:32.960
[George] Crowds of thousands knelt in the streets outside the hall to receive the blessings of the bishops as they were escorted to and from the tower.

52
00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:44.560
[George] When the jury returned a verdict of acquittal after an all-night deliberation, church bells rang across London, bonfires blazed, and an effigy of the Pope was publicly burned in Cheapside.

53
00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:52.080
[George] When the news reached James at his military camp on Hounslow Heath, the king heard his own soldiers cheering hysterically.

54
00:07:52.080 --> 00:08:00.800
[George] When James asked what the cheering was for, an officer replied, Nothing, sire, but the soldiers rejoicing that the bishops are acquitted.

55
00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:04.240
[George] James grimly murmured, Do you call that nothing?

56
00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:19.680
[George] On June 30, 1688, the day the seven bishops were acquitted, seven prominent English statesmen, the immortal seven, representing both Whig and Tory factions, dispatched a secret, coded letter to William of Orange.

57
00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:25.040
[George] Stadholder of the Dutch Republic and husband to James's eldest Protestant daughter Mary.

58
00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:32.640
[George] They invited William to invade England with an army to restore English liberties and investigate the legitimacy of the royal infant.

59
00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:42.640
[George] On November 5, 1688, the anniversary of the gunpowder plot, William's armada of four hundred ships landed at Torbay in Devon under the banner.

60
00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:45.600
[George] The Protestant religion and the liberties of England.

61
00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:50.080
[George] James marched to Salisbury to confront the invader with his superior army.

62
00:08:50.080 --> 00:09:03.480
[George] But James's spirit collapsed, plagued by severe nosebleeds and paranoia, he watched his commanders desert him one by one, including his favoured general John Churchill and his own younger daughter, Princess Anne.

63
00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:16.120
[George] Returning brokenhearted to London, James threw the great seal of England into the River Thames and attempted to flee, being caught by Kentish fishermen before William arranged for his discreet escape to France.

64
00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:23.240
[George] William of Orange's invasion fleet of November 1688 was an amphibious armada of colossal proportions.

65
00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:33.480
[George] Four hundred transport ships escorted by fifty Dutch men of war, carrying 14,000 veteran soldiers, 21,000 horses, and modern artillery.

66
00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:39.720
[George] As William landed at Brixham in Torbay, James's army at Salisbury began disintegrating.

67
00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:46.840
[George] Lord Churchill, whom James had raised from obscurity to wealth and title, deserted to William in the middle of the night.

68
00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:52.360
[George] Princess Anne slipped out of Whitehall Palace in a hackney coach to join the rebels in Nottingham.

69
00:09:52.360 --> 00:10:00.920
[George] When James learned his own daughter had turned against him, the broken father wept, God help me, even my children have forsaken me.

70
00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:09.800
[George] Welcomed at Versailles by Louis X IV with a luxurious palace at Saint Germain-Nenle, James refused to abandon his claim to the throne.

71
00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:20.280
[George] In March 1689, backed by French Golden officers, James landed in Ireland, where the Catholic majority rallied to him as their legitimate sovereign.

72
00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:33.960
[George] The Protestant garrison of Derry shut its gates against James's army, with apprentice boys shouting no surrender, enduring a horrific 105 day siege in which citizens ate rats and tallow before being relieved.

73
00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:37.960
[George] On July 1, 1690, King William I.I.

74
00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:39.080
[George] and King James I.

75
00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:44.920
[George] faced each other across the River Boyne near Drogheadda in one of the most consequential battles in European history.

76
00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:58.080
[George] William, wounded in the shoulder by a cannonball on the eve of battle, led his multinational army of 36,000 men across the river, shattering James's Franco-Irish cavalry and infantry.

77
00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:10.960
[George] James watched the defeat from a nearby hill, panicked, and fled the field, riding through the night to Dublin and sailing for France, earning the bitter Gaelic nickname Seyama's and Chaka, James the Coward.

78
00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:19.520
[George] The Battle of the Boyne on July 1, 1690, pitted the two monarchs directly against each other for the crowns of Britain and Ireland.

79
00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:33.520
[George] William, wearing an orange sash and commanding an elite multinational coalition of Dutch, Danish, Huguenot, and English troops, personally led his cavalry across the chest deep waters of the Boyne under heavy musketry fire.

80
00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:41.280
[George] James's Irish cavalry under Patrick Sarsfield fought with desperate bravery, but his ill-trained infantry was broken.

81
00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:54.480
[George] When James fled to Dublin before the battle was fully concluded, he told Lady Teoconnell that her countrymen had run away, to which the witty Irish noblewoman famously retorted, I see that your majesty hath won the race.

82
00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:57.600
[George] James sailed for France, never to return.

83
00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:00.320
[George] James spent the final decade of his life at St.

84
00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:03.840
[George] German Enlay in profound, ascetic penance.

85
00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:19.680
[George] Convinced that his loss of three kingdoms was God's righteous punishment for his youthful sins of the flesh, James wore an iron chain around his waist, scourged his back with whips, and spent hours kneeling in prayer before the blessed sacrament alongside trappist monks.

86
00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:29.760
[George] In 1692, the destruction of the French invasion fleet by the Anglo-Dutch Navy at the Battle of La Hague extinguished his last hope of a military return.

87
00:12:29.720 --> 00:12:43.320
[George] In England, the Convention Parliament had declared that James had abdicated by throwing the Great Seal into the Thames and fleeing, enacting the Bill of Rights and the Toleration Act, permanently establishing parliamentary supremacy.

88
00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:48.520
[George] On September 16, 1701, 67-year-old James I.I.

89
00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:50.440
[George] died of a brain hemorrhage at St.

90
00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:51.240
[George] German.

91
00:12:51.240 --> 00:13:03.720
[George] On his deathbed, he urged his 13-year-old son, the old pretender, never to abandon his Catholic faith for the crowns of the world, passing the torch of Jacobite rebellion to the next century.

92
00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:05.240
[George] In his final years at St.

93
00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:14.360
[George] German Enlay, James lived as a saintly penitent, writing touching spiritual advice for his young son James Francis Edward Stewart.

94
00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:23.160
[George] He fasted rigorously, washed the feet of poor men during Holy Week, and sought absolute spiritual forgiveness for his political failures.

95
00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:41.560
[George] When he died in September 1701, King Louis XIV of France visited his deathbed and promised to recognize his son as King James III of England and VIII of Scotland, igniting 60 years of Jacobite rebellions that would culminate on the bloody moor of Culloden in 1746.

96
